## Property Binding and Component Interface Guidelines

### Critical: Stencil Property Binding Syntax
Spectrum components are built with Stencil and require specific property binding syntax that differs from standard HTML attributes:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct - Stencil Property Binding
<spectrum-button 
  .buttonText=${"Click me!"}
  .variant=${"primary"}
  .size=${"base"}
  .leftIcon=${"save"}
  .showLeftIcon=${true}
  ?disabled=${false}
  ?iconOnly=${false}
/>

// ❌ Incorrect - HTML Attribute Binding (won't work)
<spectrum-button 
  label="Click me!"
  variant="primary"
  size="base"
  icon="save"
  disabled
  icon-only
/>
```

### Property Binding Rules
1. **Properties**: Use `.propertyName=${value}` syntax for all object/string/number properties
2. **Booleans**: Use `?propertyName=${boolean}` syntax for boolean properties
3. **CamelCase**: Properties are camelCase (`.buttonText`), not kebab-case (`button-text`)
4. **Value Types**: String values need `${"string"}`, booleans need `${true/false}`, numbers use `${123}`

### Common Property Binding Patterns
```typescript
// Text properties
.buttonText=${"Save Changes"}
.placeholder=${"Enter search term"}
.label=${"Form Label"}

// Enum/option properties  
.variant=${"primary"}
.size=${"large"}
.iconPosition=${"left"}

// Boolean properties
?disabled=${true}
?expanded=${false}
?outline=${true}

// Numeric properties
.maxItems=${10}
.delay=${300}

// Object/Array properties
.items=${[{id: 1, name: 'Item'}]}
.config=${{theme: 'dark', size: 'lg'}}
```

## Component Interface Investigation (MANDATORY)

### Read Actual Component Files Before Creating Stories
You MUST read the component's `.tsx` file to understand actual properties. Never assume property names:

1. **Read the component's `.tsx` file** from `packages/core/src/components/spectrum-[component]/`
2. **Verify property names** from the `@Prop()` decorators
3. **Check event names** from the `@Event()` decorators  
4. **Understand prop types** and validation rules
5. **Note any special property combinations** (e.g., iconOnly + showLeftIcon)

### Property Discovery Process
```bash
# Navigate to component directory
cd packages/core/src/components/spectrum-[component]

# Read the main component file
cat spectrum-[component].tsx

# Look for @Prop() declarations to find actual property names
grep -A 2 "@Prop()" spectrum-[component].tsx

# Look for @Event() declarations to find event names
grep -A 2 "@Event()" spectrum-[component].tsx
```

### Real Examples of Property Name Mismatches
These are actual discrepancies we discovered:

```typescript
// spectrum-button component actual vs assumed properties:

// ❌ WRONG Assumptions:
label: "Click me!"           // Wrong property name
icon: "save"                 // Wrong - component uses separate left/right icons
size: "medium"               // Wrong - component uses "base" not "medium"
size: "x-large"              // Wrong - component uses "lg" not "x-large"
variant: "error"             // Wrong - component uses "danger" not "error"

// ✅ CORRECT Actual Properties from spectrum-button.tsx:
buttonText: "Click me!"      // Correct property name
leftIcon: "save"             // Correct - separate left/right icon properties
showLeftIcon: true           // Required to actually display the icon
size: "base"                 // Correct size value (options: "sm", "base", "lg")
variant: "danger"            // Correct variant name
```

### Critical Component Interface Patterns
Some components have special property requirements:

```typescript
// spectrum-button: Icon-only buttons need BOTH properties
?iconOnly=${true}
.showLeftIcon=${true}        // Required even for icon-only buttons
.leftIcon=${"add"}

// spectrum-button: Icon with text needs specific properties
.buttonText=${"Save"}
.leftIcon=${"save"}
.showLeftIcon=${true}        // Must be true to display icon

// spectrum-button: Available sizes are limited
.size=${"sm"}               // ✅ Available
.size=${"base"}             // ✅ Available (default)
.size=${"lg"}               // ✅ Available
.size=${"medium"}           // ❌ Not available
.size=${"x-large"}          // ❌ Not available
```

## Icon Font Requirements

### Material Symbols Font Loading
Spectrum components use Material Symbols for icons. Stories must account for font loading:

```typescript
const meta: Meta<SpectrumButton> = {
  title: 'Spectrum/Components/SpectrumButton',
  component: 'spectrum-button',
  parameters: {
    docs: {
      description: {
        component: 'Requires Material Symbols font to be loaded for icon display. Icons may not appear immediately if font is still loading.'
      }
    }
  }
};
```

### Icon Display Requirements
```typescript
// Icon-only button (requires both iconOnly and show properties)
?iconOnly=${true}
.showLeftIcon=${true}
.leftIcon=${"add"}

// Button with left icon and text
.buttonText=${"Save"}
.leftIcon=${"save"}
.showLeftIcon=${true}        // Critical: icon won't show without this

// Button with right icon and text  
.buttonText=${"Next"}
.rightIcon=${"arrow_forward"}
.showRightIcon=${true}       // Critical: icon won't show without this

// Verify icon names from Material Symbols documentation
.leftIcon=${"format_underline"}  // ✅ Correct spelling
.leftIcon=${"format_underlined"} // ❌ Incorrect - missing 'd'
```

### Font Loading in Storybook
Ensure Material Symbols font is available in your Storybook environment:
- Font should be loaded in Storybook's HTML head
- Icons may not display immediately during font loading
- Document this behavior in component descriptions

## Story Development Workflow

### Pre-Development Checklist
Before creating any component stories:

- [ ] Read the actual component `.tsx` file thoroughly
- [ ] Document all `@Prop()` properties with their exact names and types
- [ ] Document all `@Event()` events with their exact names and payloads
- [ ] Identify any special property combinations or requirements
- [ ] Test icon display if component uses icons
- [ ] Verify Material Symbols font is loaded in Storybook

### Development Process
1. **Component Analysis**: Read `.tsx` file, document actual interface
2. **Property Mapping**: Create mapping of actual vs expected property names
3. **Icon Testing**: Test icon display with proper show/hide properties
4. **Binding Validation**: Ensure all properties use correct Stencil syntax
5. **Story Creation**: Build stories using verified component interface
6. **Integration Testing**: Test stories in Storybook to ensure proper display

### Post-Development Validation
After creating stories:

- [ ] All controls work correctly in Storybook playground
- [ ] Icons display properly (not just empty spaces)
- [ ] All component variants render as expected
- [ ] Event handlers fire with correct payloads
- [ ] TypeScript compilation succeeds without errors
- [ ] Component displays match design specifications

## Common Issues and Solutions

### Property Binding Issues
```typescript
// ❌ PROBLEM: Using HTML attributes instead of Stencil properties
<spectrum-button label="Click" variant="primary" size="large" disabled>

// ✅ SOLUTION: Use proper Stencil property binding
<spectrum-button .buttonText=${"Click"} .variant=${"primary"} .size=${"lg"} ?disabled=${true}>
```

### Icon Display Issues
```typescript
// ❌ PROBLEM: Icon not showing despite setting icon property
<spectrum-button .leftIcon=${"save"} ?iconOnly=${true}>

// ✅ SOLUTION: Include required show property
<spectrum-button .leftIcon=${"save"} .showLeftIcon=${true} ?iconOnly=${true}>
```

### Property Name Issues
```typescript
// ❌ PROBLEM: Using assumed property names
<spectrum-component .label=${"Text"} .size=${"medium"}>

// ✅ SOLUTION: Use actual property names from component interface
<spectrum-component .componentText=${"Text"} .size=${"base"}>
```

### TypeScript Interface Issues
```typescript
// ❌ PROBLEM: Using generic or assumed interfaces
interface ButtonArgs {
  label: string;
  size: 'small' | 'medium' | 'large';
}

// ✅ SOLUTION: Import and use actual component interface
import type { SpectrumButton } from '../../../../core/src/components/spectrum-button/spectrum-button';

interface ButtonArgs extends SpectrumButton {
  // Additional story-specific properties if needed
}
```

### Event Handling Issues
```typescript
// ❌ PROBLEM: Generic event handling without action validation
@buttonClick=${(e) => console.log(e.detail)}

// ✅ SOLUTION: Properly typed event handling with action validation
@buttonClick=${(e: CustomEvent<ButtonActionPayload>) => {
  console.log('Action:', e.detail.action);
  console.log('Payload:', e.detail);
}}
```

